  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   The central idea is to improve upon the current Erds minimum overlap problem constant by systematically exploring different representations of the step function with varying discretization levels. Unlike the coordinate descent and SLSQP methods used previously, this approach will:\n   - Use randomized quasi-periodic search to avoid local optima\n   - Explore multi-resolution representations by adaptive grid refinement\n   - Implement a directed search guided by feature density analysis\n   - Perform post-optimization with coordinate-wise Newton-like updates\n\n   The current code primarily uses gradient-free methods, which may get trapped in local optima. We'll introduce:\n   - A multi-stage optimization strategy to systematically explore the space\n   - Cross-level feature consistency checks to preserve structure when resizing\n   - Strategic randomization with deterministic restart protocols\n   - Cost-benefit guided restart frequency based on estimated time remaining\n\n   The solitary bottleneck is the high dimensionality and non-convex nature of the optimization landscape. This approach divides the optimization into four interconnected phases to better navigate this terrain.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   We represent the step function h:[0,2]\u1eac[0,1] as:\n     - h: (n+1) points with sum h[i] = n/2 (after scaling)\n     - The overlap integral C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\n   where:\n     - The domain [0,2] is discretized into n+1 points with spacing 2/n\n     - np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') computes the overlap contributions at all possible shifts\n     - Final C_5 = (2/n)*max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full'))\n\n   Constraints:\n     - 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n     - sum(h)*dx = 1 with dx = 2/n\n\n   Optimization Objective:\n     - Minimize final C_5 value\n\n   Helper functions:\n     - project_feasible(h,n): Enforces box and integral constraints\n     - exact_coord_min(h,n): Computes optimal coordinate update for CD method\n     - compute_c5(h,n): Computes 2.0/n * max correlation\n\n   Search Strategy:\n     - Initial constructions:\n         * Base representation from initial_h_values if available\n         * Random projection of uniform prior (0.5) to initial resolution\n         * Radially symmetric shape from known discrepancy-minimizing functions\n     - Optimization sequence:\n         1. Local refinement with projected gradient descent\n         2. Random walks with correlation-driven selection\n         3. Strategic coordinate descent updates\n         4. Multi-resolution optimization\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   Phase 1: Plan execution (duration: 10% of budget)\n     - Initialize n_values list (500, 600, 800, 1000, 1200) with specified parent\n     - Set up random generators with unique seeds per candidate\n     - Compute baseline C_5 from existing parent\n\n   Phase 2: Multi-resolution exploration (duration: 70% of budget)\n     - Apply exponential random-walk based optimization (ERWO) to each n in descending order\n     - Evaluate each candidate with compute_c5 after each parameter update\n     - Maintain Pareto front of (n, C_5) pairs\n     - Break early when improvement plateau reached\n\n   Phase 3: Focused refinement (duration: 15% of budget)\n     - Extract feature map from best estimate (peak locations, transition points)\n     - Perform coordinate-wise Newton updates based on local gradient analysis\n     - Apply multi-level interpolation at 10 additional resolution points\n\n   Phase 4: Cost-benefit adaptive controller (runs throughout)\n     - Monitor time/landscape statistics to determine restart frequency\n     - Periodically compare solutions using cross-resolution embedding\n     - Switch to exploitation strategy if landscape flatens\n     - Return best solution before budget depletion\n\n   Coordinate representation:\n     - Primary form: Opportunistic interval tree representation with adaptive discretization\n     - Secondary validation: Functional embedding with Fourier basis\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Default Strategy (Core method):\n        init_candidates = 3\n        exploration_rate = 0.7\n        refinement_depth = 10\n        coord_descent_freq = 20\n\n   - Feature Alignment Variation:\n        Tracks activation pattern stopping criterion\n        Requires implementing sensitivity map calculation\n        S\u9002\u5b9c\u4e8e complex landscapes but computational cost higher\n\n   - Multi-peak Handling Variation:\n        Diversity objectives: \n          1. Cauchy-smoothed losses\n          2. Entropy-based diversity score\n        Requires separate population tracking\n\n   - Parameter adaptation:\n        Mutation rate adjustment based on hamming distance to parent\n        Learning rate warmup and cooldown phases\n\n   - The variations trade off between exploration guarantees and computational efficiency:\n        Core variation focuses on balance between exploration and exploitation\n        Feature Alignment variation offers superior multi-modal handling\n        Multi-peak variation provides robustness to optimization traps\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   Dominant operations:\n     - Correlation computation: O(n\u00b2)\n     - Constraint projection: O(n)\n     - ERWO updates: O(n)\n\n   Estimation:\n     - n=500: ~0.5s per ERWO iteration\n     - n=1000: ~2.0s per ERWO iteration\n     - ERWO typically finds C_5 improvement at 75% of resolution\n\n   Adaptive budget allocation:\n     - Maintain 15% buffer for unexpected performance drops\n     - Check progress every 30s (target: at least 80% improvement in correlation)\n\n   Worst-case handling:\n     - If no improvement for 3 consecutive ERWO generations, switch to diversity-driven restart\n     - Time warnings trigger solution archiving and budget extension requests (if available)\n\n   Resolution transfer mechanism:\n     - For resizing between n_old and n_new:\n          1. Compute alignment map between resolution levels\n          2. Use soft-thresholded interpolation (lambda=0.1 to 0.3)\n          3. Maintain feature continuity through all scale levels\n     - Fallback maintains validity if interpolation fails\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   Output checks:\n     - 0 <= h_vals[i] <= 1 for all i\n     - abs(sum(h_vals)*dx - 1) < 1e-5 (with dx=2/n)\n     - C_5 computation uses same correlation method as base task\n\n   Verification:\n     - Final candidate passes score consistency check with redundant computation\n     - Feature map recovers key characteristics from parent solution\n     - Final solution has evaluation date/time stamp (though not strictly required)\n\n   Final ritual:\n     - Project final solution to 600 resolution (parent scale)\n     - Recompute C_5 through central processing\n     - Return tuple(h_vals_refined, final_c5, n)\n\n   Implementation validation:\n     - Test compute_c5 on dummy solution (post-initialization)\n     - Ensure project_feasible maintains integral constraint\n     - Verify coordinate descent respects constraints (via assertions)\n\n   Checking procedure:\n     - Evaluate solution on finest grid first\n     - Compare to candidate using matched resolution\n     - Compute gradient check for all-but-last parameter\n     - Maintain sparse Jacobian tracking",
